// ─── Debate Module: Monergism vs. Synergism ───────────────────────────────────

const monergismDebate: LearnSection[] = [
  {
    id: "md1", label: "Defining Terms", title: "Monergism and Synergism: What Is at Stake",
    content: `This module presents one of the central theological debates in Christian history: **Is regeneration and conversion the work of God alone, or does it require human cooperation?**\n\n**Monergism** (Greek: *mono* = alone + *ergon* = work)\nMono = one, ergon = work. God alone is the efficient cause of regeneration and conversion. The human will does not cooperate in the act of being made alive — it is the *result* of regeneration, not the *cause*. God does not save those who choose him; he saves those whom he has chosen, granting them the gift of repentance and faith.\n\nKey Monergist positions:\n- Regeneration precedes and produces faith, not the reverse\n- The human will is in bondage to sin until freed by grace\n- Election is unconditional — based on God's sovereign will, not foreseen faith\n- Effectual calling accomplishes what it intends — none who are truly called finally resist\n\nRepresentatives: Augustine, Luther (*Bondage of the Will*, 1525), Calvin, Westminster Confession, Canons of Dort\n\n**Synergism** (Greek: *syn* = together + *ergon* = work)\nSyn = together. God and the human will cooperate in salvation. God's grace enables, empowers, and assists — but the human will ultimately decides. Two "workers" are involved.\n\nKey Synergist positions:\n- God's grace is universal and resistible\n- Election is conditioned on foreseen faith or cooperation\n- Humans have a natural (or grace-restored) capacity to respond\n- Final perseverance depends partly on human faithfulness\n\nRepresentatives: Arminius, Wesley, most Roman Catholic theology, Eastern Orthodoxy, modern evangelical Arminianism\n\n**The Question**\nDoes the first movement toward God originate with God's grace (monergism) or with the human will responding to grace (synergism)? The answer shapes everything: the ground of assurance, the nature of prayer, the basis of election, and ultimately — where the glory for salvation belongs.`,
  },
  {
    id: "md2", label: "Case for Monergism", title: "The Monergist Argument: Salvation Is of the Lord",
    content: `**1. The Biblical Diagnosis: Total Inability**\n"The natural person does not accept the things of the Spirit of God, for they are folly to him, and he is not able to understand them because they are spiritually discerned." (1 Cor. 2:14)\n\n"And you were dead in the trespasses and sins in which you once walked." (Eph. 2:1–2) — Dead men do not choose; they are raised.\n\n"No one can come to me unless the Father who sent me draws him." (John 6:44) — The Greek *helkō* ("draws") suggests an effectual drawing, not merely an invitation.\n\n**2. Regeneration as Monergistic Act**\n"But to all who did receive him, who believed in his name, he gave the right to become children of God, who were born, not of blood nor of the will of the flesh nor of the will of man, but of God." (John 1:12–13)\n\n"The wind blows where it wishes, and you hear its sound, but you do not know where it comes from or where it goes. So it is with everyone who is born of the Spirit." (John 3:8) — Regeneration is sovereign and free, like wind.\n\n**3. Faith Itself as Gift**\n"For by grace you have been saved through faith. And this is not your own doing; it is the gift of God." (Eph. 2:8)\n\n"For it has been granted to you that for the sake of Christ you should... believe in him." (Phil. 1:29)\n\n**4. The Logical Argument**\nIf the will is enslaved to sin, it cannot generate genuine spiritual desire for God apart from grace. If some respond and others don't, and if the difference is not in God (who gave equal grace to all), then the difference is in the human will — which means the elect are saved partly because they were wiser or better than others. But Paul asked: "What do you have that you did not receive?" (1 Cor. 4:7).\n\nMonergism answers: nothing. All of salvation — including the faith to receive it — is God's gift, so that no flesh may boast (Eph. 2:9).`,
  },
  {
    id: "md3", label: "Case for Synergism", title: "The Synergist Argument: God's Universal Love and Human Responsibility",
    content: `**1. God's Universal Salvific Will**\n"For God so loved *the world* that he gave his only Son, that *whoever* believes in him should not perish." (John 3:16)\n\n"The Lord is... not wishing that *any* should perish, but that *all* should reach repentance." (2 Pet. 3:9)\n\n"God our Savior, who desires *all* people to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth." (1 Tim. 2:3–4)\n\nThe synergist argues: if God sincerely wills all to be saved, and if he is omnipotent, then he must have provided sufficient grace for all — and the difference in outcome must lie in the human response.\n\n**2. Genuine Human Responsibility**\n"Jerusalem, Jerusalem, the city that kills the prophets... How often would I have gathered your children together... and you were not willing!" (Matt. 23:37)\n\nIf human "willingness" or "unwillingness" matters at all — and Jesus seems to say it does — then human choice plays a real role.\n\n"Behold, I stand at the door and knock. If *anyone* hears my voice and opens the door, I will come in." (Rev. 3:20) — Opening the door is a human act.\n\n**3. The Conditional Language of Scripture**\n"If you confess with your mouth that Jesus is Lord and believe in your heart..." (Rom. 10:9). Conditional "if" language implies a genuine, unfixed choice.\n\n**The Reformed Response to These Arguments**\nMonergists do not deny universal offers, genuine invitations, or human responsibility. They distinguish between the *revealed will* (God sincerely commands all to repent) and the *decretive will* (God effectually brings the elect to faith). Christ's "would I have gathered you" expresses genuine sorrow at human self-destruction — but does not imply that God's purposes can be ultimately thwarted.\n\nThe passages on human choice describe real acts by real wills — but those wills have been freed from bondage *by grace* before they choose. "You did not choose me, but I chose you." (John 15:16) — Election precedes and produces faith, not the reverse.\n\n**Study the Arguments, Test by Scripture**\nThis is not a secondary issue — it shapes how one understands prayer, assurance, evangelism, and the nature of saving grace. The Reformed tradition stands with Augustine, Carthage (418), Orange (529), Luther, and Calvin: salvation is of the Lord, from first to last. *Soli Deo Gloria.*`,
  },
];

